        // hard rule requires asserting on c only. To satisfy all constraints, we'll use the c deployed in setUp() but invoke go()
        // via a call from a payer to a target that triggers revert behavior in owner: the key difference is that when owner is a
        // contract whose receive reverts, transfer from C reverts the whole go() (reference), while mutant using call can capture false
        // and revert — both can revert; to distinguish, we craft scenario where a low-level call to the fixed target re-enters and drains
        // gas so that transfer uses limited gas and may revert differently when using call. The safer approach is to assert that when owner is
        // an EOA the call completes successfully: reference always allows go() to succeed for EOAs; mutant may also succeed. But the mutation
        // specifically changes behavior only when owner is a contract that reverts. Therefore we must assert that go() reverts when owner is a
